59
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:49,580
But I did something about
the sleepwalking idea.

60
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:52,760
That night, before bed, I set my phone

61
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:55,300
on the little table by
the couch and started a

62
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:57,560
voice recording, and I
let it run all night.

63
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:00,920
In the morning, I almost
didn’t listen to it.

64
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:03,280
Seven hours of a quiet apartment didn’t

65
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:05,660
seem worth the time.
But on my lunch break,

66
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:07,960
I opened it and scrolled through the audio

67
00:03:07,960 --> 00:03:10,500
waveform, looking for
anything that wasn’t a

68
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:12,940
flat line. Most of it was just the hum of

69
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:17,700
the refrigerator and the radiator ticking.
Then, at three-eleven in the morning, the

70
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:24,640
line started to move. I put my
earbuds in and pressed play. At first,

71
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:28,080
it was just a creak, the
long, low creak an old

72
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:29,820
floorboard makes when weight settles on

73
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:34,020
